condition_name: Urinary Schistosomiasis
condition_slug: urinary-schistosomiasis
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "A common parasitic infection of the urinary tract caused by a body fluke, Schistosoma haematobium. Acquired while bathing/wading in infected water. Endemic in many parts of Africa and gets to the urinary tract through the blood vessels after penetrating the skin."
clinical_features[3]:
  - type: Soon after penetration of the skin
    features[1]: Pricking sensation and itching (cercarial dermatitis)
  - type: Four weeks later
    features[4]: Intermittent fever,malaise,urticaria,cough
  - type: 6 - 24 months later
    features[2]: "Intermittent, painless terminal haematuria (may be total)","Symptoms of bladder irritability: dysuria, frequency, urgency, strangury"
investigations[6]: Urine examination for schistosomal ova,"Cystoscopy: tubercles, sandy patches, nodules, ulcers",Plain abdominal radiograph (KUB),Intravenous urogram,Serological tests,Full Blood Count
treatment:
  goals[2]: To eradicate the fluke and ova,Prevent complications
  non_drug[0]:
  drug[3]: "Praziquantel: Adult: Single oral dose of 50 mg/kg","Child over 4 years: 20 mg/kg orally, repeated after 4 – 6 hours","In S. japonicum infection, 20 mg/kg 3 times daily for one day after initial dose"
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[5]: Nausea,epigastric pain,pruritus,headache,dizziness
  supportive_measures[0]:
differential_diagnoses[3]: Tuberculous cystitis,Abacterial cystitis,Bladder carcinoma
complications[5]: Bladder fibrosis and contracture,Ureteral stricture,Urethral stricture,Bladder calculi,Bladder cancer
prevention[4]: Provision of and access to pipe-borne water,Improvement in socio-economic conditions,Mass chemotherapy in endemic areas,Eradicating the intermediate hosts (water snails)
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null